  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft remains dominated by a strong high \npressure system over the central CONUS. Despite this, a lower/mid-\nlevel low pressure system is present near the Nebraska/Colorado \nborder as a extensive line of storms have fired in eastern Colorado. \nExcluding the continued preset of heat, the storm potential is the \nforecast focus.\n\nThe storms have already produced a slew of wind gusts from 60-70 mph \nin Colorado. The line of storms have breached the Kansas border and \none 50 mph wind gust has been recorded. It will need to be monitored \nif the storms can strengthen the cold pool. The same CAMs have the \nstorms weakening as they enter Kansas and beyond, so the severe \nwindow is expected to stay near the border. However, if the storms \noverperform, gusts of up to 60 mph are possible as far east as \nGarden City. Modeled soundings have a gradient of dropping CAPE and \nclimbing CIN with the eastward extend. That said, PW values of 1.5\" \nand DCAPE of 1500 J/KG will still be enough to produce a lingering \nsevere threat in the strongest storms. How quickly they die off will \nbe closely monitored. \n\nWednesday is forecast to be marginally cooler than Tuesday at around \n3 degrees cooler CWA wide. Realistically, the drop will not be \nnoticed as it will still feel hot as the hot summer July stretch \ncontinues. A heat advisory will be issued for Clark, Comanche, Pratt \nand Barber counties from 1 to 9 PM. Traditional heat precautions are \nadvised including hydration and cooling breaks. CAMs have hinted at \nisolated thunderstorm development Wednesday evening, but the \ncontinuity has been low. Ensembles keep precipitation chances below \n20% with the best of these chances in the western zones. \n\nThursday is more of the same with very hot highs and marginal storm \nchances. Highs are forecast mostly in the 100-105 degree range with \nmuch of the southern counties at or above apparent temperatures of \n105 degrees. A Heat Advisory is expected to be warranted for some of \nthe counties Thursday. Long-ranged CAMs have an MCS descending into \ncentral Kansas from Nebraska. Depending on the formation and path, \nit may clip the northeastern counties in the forecast area with \nsevere wind gusts. More time will be needed to monitor the \nforecasted development. Some later redevelopment may initiate \nconvection in far SW Kansas late Thursday, but severe impacts are \nnot expected at this time. \n\nA drier stretch is then expected to take hold into the weekend and \ninto next week. Temperatures will also be a tad cooler with \nhighs mostly in the upper 90s as opposed to the 100 degree days \nof this week. No weather headlines are forecast to be needed at \nthis time.",
